    {
      "id": "DLM-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Choosing a short integral vector seen by the dual covector",
      "anchor": "inputs-dual-test",
      "statement": "If f attains the dual minimum of the corrected form, the least squared input length m of any v with f(v)≠0 satisfies m≤12C_T/λ∨(h). There is a place w with |f_w(v₀)|≥1 and a vector y representing f_w in the original metric. For b=y* h_w y one has 4bm≥1.",
      "hypotheses": "L totally complex, N≥1, h positive at all infinite places, μ a smoothing of λ∨. C_T is a covering constant such that every vector in the archimedean space is within squared distance C_T/λ∨(h) of an integral vector.",
      "definitions": [
        "A covector is represented by an integral coefficient column and acts by the bilinear dot product. Its dual metric has inverse-transpose matrix.",
        "The metric representative at w is y=h_w⁻¹ conjugate(f_w). The proof picks one place where the image of the nonzero algebraic integer f(v₀) has absolute value at least one."
      ],
